data language_target_registry_totality_law: String = "NO INVISIBLE REMAINDER (operator requirement C, 2026-08-04). This registry may not read as complete because a few specimen rows exist. Every target-bearing surface this repository currently carries is EITHER joined to a registered subject OR appears here as an exact row with no identity, which the join kernel then reports in the missing-identity class. A surface that is simply absent from these lists is the one state the wall cannot see, which is why the population definition below is declared rather than implied, and why the TargetVocabulary rows carry an explicit scaffold note rather than a silent transcription. The registry today registers THREE subjects — Bash, externally grounded, plus the .dag language and the controlled-English medium, project-grounded — while the census carries forty surfaces, so most rows below are deliberately unidentified. That is the honest starting state, not a defect in the wall. WHAT 'ACCOUNTED FOR' NOW MEANS PRECISELY (corrected 2026-08-04, review relayed through the operator): the accounting is an EXACT equality, identified rows plus reported missing-identity rows equals the census, and it is separate from the integrity wall that requires zero unknown-target, ambiguous, duplicate-subject and duplicate-locus violations. The single predicate these replace summed both classes and compared them with a <=, which stayed green with malformed input present and would have stayed green against an empty violation list."

data language_target_registry_vocabulary_scaffold_note: String = "DECLARED SCAFFOLD, with its dissolution trigger — the TargetVocabulary rows below are HAND-DECLARED, and they were meant to be derived. The intent was to read v2.lens.realization_vocabulary_containment's target_ast_vocab_modules and target_ast_vocab_module_prefixes directly, so a module added to the lens would appear here automatically as an unidentified surface and the second-hardcoded-roster fork would close by construction. THAT IMPORT DOES NOT COMPILE TODAY, and the blocker is not in this module: the lens fails to resolve fold_list and list_snoc_item from v2.std.algebra even when compiled from its OWN source root as the entry — measured 2026-08-04. That is the Class B pool-membership defect DESIGN's import-strip open thread describes: a module's bare cross-module references resolve only when some unrelated import elsewhere in the assembled closure has already dragged the target into the pool, so the lens resolves inside the whole-corpus compile and not in a narrow closure. Transcribing the two lists here would be the workaround DESIGN section 5 forbids — an unmarked second representation of the very roster this registry exists to unfork — so they are declared as a counted scaffold instead. DISSOLVE-ON: the lens resolves under a narrow closure (Class B fix), at which point these rows become the fold over its lists; and ultimately the reverse direction, the lens deriving its confined set FROM this registry, which is the prerequisite edge from the support-registry obligation to the no-new-debt-gate obligation in the governing strategy contract, and is not done here. Those obligations are identified in a private strategy overlay rather than in this repository, so they are described by role and not named — publishing an overlay identifier is an explicit act under that repository's opt-in-public model, and it has not been authorised."

data language_target_registry_population_definition: String = "Target-bearing surfaces enumerated from six declared authorities: (1) StdLanguageCatalogue — std.languages Language rows; (2) TargetModelDeclaration — committed TargetModel-bearing language modules; (3) EmitHostExecution — tools.emit_host_transport per-language smoke fns; (4) TargetVocabulary — the confined module list and prefix list v2.lens.realization_vocabulary_containment enforces; (5) RuntimeChecker — external toolchains a support claim would execute against; (6) LegacyFrontier — gunbc.plans.language_target_self_host_frontier's prose rows. ALL SIX authorities are HAND-DECLARED at this revision — authority 4 was intended to be derived and is a counted scaffold with a stated trigger (language_target_registry_vocabulary_scaffold_note); nothing in this module is derived from the live tree. GRAIN, because it decides whether a row can be checked: a locus is either a real module path or a declared PREFIX over several (the extdeps.languages family paths cover multiple modules each, and v2.workflow.bash is a prefix over two, matching the containment lens's own grain). Distinguishing a legitimate prefix from a path that names nothing requires testing exact-match and prefix-match separately — the instrument fierce-heron-301 used to find three phantom rows among nine legitimate ones (2026-08-04)."

data language_target_registry_maximum_conclusion: String = "MAXIMUM CONCLUSION — what a green accounting or integrity check here does and does not establish. IT ESTABLISHES: every surface in the declared population above is accounted for, each either joined to a registered subject or reported as an exact unidentified row, and that no malformed join input is present; IT DOES NOT ESTABLISH that the declared population is COMPLETE with respect to the repository. All six authorities are hand-declared, so a target-bearing surface nobody wrote a row for is invisible to every assertion over this module — the failure mode loyal-ant-382 measured at 43 percent under-selection on a text-keyed census and this session reproduced at 100 percent on the ShellCommand sink. NOR DOES IT ESTABLISH THAT A DECLARED ROW NAMES A REAL SURFACE: three census rows were found on 2026-08-04 to be file paths transcribed as module paths, naming modules that do not exist, and no assertion in this module could have caught them because the population is the thing being asserted over. Closing both directions needs a Node-tree observer deriving the population from the real module tree, which is the residue-census obligation and is NOT built here. No count in this module may be quoted as a corpus claim."

data registry_revision_note: String = "WHY THERE IS NO PINNED REVISION STAMP HERE, removed rather than corrected (2026-08-04). An earlier revision of this module carried language_target_registry_subject_revision as a hand-written commit SHA, on the reasoning that a row set without its revision is a measurement pretending to be a standing fact. The reasoning was right and the mechanism was self-defeating in two ways. FIRST, IT WAS ALREADY FALSE: the stamp named the commit current when it was typed, and the row set then changed in four subsequent commits — including the one taking the registry from one target to three — so the declaration asserted an enumeration that had not held for some time. A review approved it as anchored honestly, which is the tell that a stale stamp reads as provenance rather than as the claim it actually is. SECOND, AND DECISIVELY, IT CANNOT BE FIXED BY UPDATING: pinning the SHA of the commit that contains the pin is self-referential, so any such stamp is stale by at least one commit BY CONSTRUCTION, and each update re-copies a number from the tree — the measure() == measure() collapse DESIGN section 5 names. The rows ARE anchored, by the thing that already anchors them: git records exactly which revision this file held which rows, at line grain, without anyone maintaining it. A second hand-kept representation of that fact is the section 3 duplication this registry exists to remove, applied to the registry itself."

data bash_subject_ref: DeclarationRef = DeclarationRef {
  module_path: "extdeps.languages.bash.subject",
  decl_name: "gnu_bash",
  field: WholeDeclaration
}

data d0_population_blocker_note: String = "D0 — THE COMPLETE TARGET UNIVERSE — IS NOT REGISTERED HERE, AND THE REASON IS THE CONSTRUCTION WALL ITSELF. A registration derives its subject from a REQUIRED TargetGrounding, so an ungrounded target is unwritable (target_grounding_law). External grounding is an ExternalModelScope, and a scope carries at least one real upstream citation by construction. Therefore registering TypeScript, C, C++, Verilog, SPICE, Python, Go, Java, Kotlin, Swift, LLVM IR, WebAssembly, PTX, machine code, Lean, SQL, HTML, CSS, ECMAScript, JSON, YAML and Markdown requires FIRST authoring one cited subject declaration each — the same file this branch created for Bash — because NONE of them has one today. That is the measured state, not an obstacle invented here: Bash was uncited until this branch, and the same sweep that found it found no subject declaration for any other target. Registering them without grounding would mean either fabricating citations or reopening the wall, and both are refused. The .dag language and controlled English take ProjectGrounding instead and need no external citation; both are ALREADY REGISTERED in this module, so what remains for D0 is exactly the external population. SIZING, so the remaining work is countable rather than vague: roughly twenty external subject declarations, each a new dag/extdeps scope carrier requiring its own roster enrollment, each needing a citation verified against the real upstream rather than recalled. That population is in flight on the stacked D0 branch. DISSOLVE-ON: those subjects land and this note deletes with the rows it explains."

data project_grounded_targets_note: String = "The two PROJECT-grounded targets, and they are here for a reason beyond being cheap: ProjectGrounding had zero inhabitants, so an entire arm of the grounding coproduct was unexercised. These are also the two rows that could not have existed under a universal external subject carrier — the .dag language and the controlled-English medium are this project's OWN targets, with no independently governed upstream to cite, and forcing them through an external scope would have made them claim a governance they do not have. Their authority declarations are real and symbolic: dag_item_forms is the .dag surface-form authority, FormalSentence the controlled-English one. Neither carries an extdeps_model_scope and neither should. They are also now PROJECTABLE, which they were not while the join key was an external subject reference — the neutral DeclarationRef key is what lets one projection roster span both grounding arms instead of silently serving only external targets."

data registered_language_targets: List<LanguageTargetRegistration> = [
  LanguageTargetRegistration {
    grounding: ExternalGrounding { scope: gnu_bash_model_scope },
    disposition: TargetActive
  },
  LanguageTargetRegistration {
    grounding: ProjectGrounding {
      authority: DeclarationRef {
        module_path: "extdeps.languages.dag.syntax",
        decl_name: "dag_item_forms",
        field: WholeDeclaration
      }
    },
    disposition: TargetActive
  },
  LanguageTargetRegistration {
    grounding: ProjectGrounding {
      authority: DeclarationRef {
        module_path: "v2.extdeps.languages.english",
        decl_name: "FormalSentence",
        field: WholeDeclaration
      }
    },
    disposition: TargetParked {
      reason: "controlled-English medium: bounded formal-subset profile, P3 priority; registered so the strategy row stays visible while implementation has not begun"
    }
  }
]

data dag_subject_ref: DeclarationRef = DeclarationRef {
  module_path: "extdeps.languages.dag.syntax",
  decl_name: "dag_item_forms",
  field: WholeDeclaration
}

data english_subject_ref: DeclarationRef = DeclarationRef {
  module_path: "v2.extdeps.languages.english",
  decl_name: "FormalSentence",
  field: WholeDeclaration
}

fn registered_target_count() -> Int {
  fold(registered_language_targets, init: 0, f: fn(a, _r) { a + 1 })
}

fn unidentified_decl_row(
  authority: LanguageTargetSurfaceAuthority,
  module_path: String,
  decl_name: String
) -> LanguageTargetSurfaceRow {
  LanguageTargetSurfaceRow {
    authority: authority,
    surface: SurfaceDeclaration {
      ref: DeclarationRef {
        module_path: module_path,
        decl_name: decl_name,
        field: WholeDeclaration
      }
    },
    target: none
  }
}

fn unidentified_module_row(
  authority: LanguageTargetSurfaceAuthority,
  module_path: String
) -> LanguageTargetSurfaceRow {
  LanguageTargetSurfaceRow {
    authority: authority,
    surface: SurfaceModule { module_path: module_path },
    target: none
  }
}

